# Task Handoff (host-side)

## When to Use

- The **host CLI session** (the orchestrator role reading `AGENTS.md`) is
  about to delegate a subtask to a specialized subagent.
- You need to verify a delegation packet covers the 12 Required Minimum
  fields plus the right expansion blocks for the task tag.
- A subagent needs the canonical Acceptance Checklist or Reporting Template
  (consumer side) — the skill body is the source of truth that
  subagent templates point to.

## Do Not Use

- For subagent-to-subagent delegation. Subagents are **executors**; if a
  subtask exceeds their owned paths, they `return-to-orchestrator` and the
  host composes the next assignment.
- For replacing the inline 12-field fail-closed guard in subagent templates.
  The guard stays inline so subagents can reject malformed assignments even
  when this skill is not loaded.

## Hard Rules

1. **Host-only composition.** Only the host CLI session composes packets
   using this skill. Subagents consume packets; they do not author them.
2. **Required Minimum 12 fields, every assignment** (the 12 required-minimum fields).
   `Task`, `Source plan`, `Owned paths`, `Read-only paths`, `Relevant gates`,
   `Constraints`, `Dependencies / wave`, `Required approvals`, `Runtime format
   target`, `Expected output`, `Context freshness`, `Lossiness`.
3. **Pass loaded-skill evidence.** When the host has loaded this skill for
   the current turn, include `Skills Referenced: task-handoff loaded=true`
   in the packet so subagents know they may rely on pointer-only templates.
4. **Fail-closed.** If any Required Minimum field is missing, the subagent
   returns one consolidated `question_request` and stops before any gated
   write. Do not pressure it forward.
5. **No subagent re-delegation through this skill.** Subagents return to
   the host when scope exceeds their owned paths.

## Procedure (host side)

1. **Pick the task tag** from `AGENTS.md` › Task-Type Routing Map
   (`code-edit`, `security-write`, `bug-hunting`, `mcp-write`, `release`,
   `docs-only`, `bug-fix`, `replication`, `instruction-memory-audit`,
   `code-change-build-gate`, etc.).
2. **Pick the packet form** from the Recommended Packet Form table below.
3. **Fill the Required Minimum 12 fields** from your current plan and the
   Directory Architecture. Set `Context freshness: recent` when `AGENTS.md`
   was loaded this turn.
4. **Add expansion blocks** for the picked task tag (Goal & Definition of
   Done, Scope, Context Packet, File Inventory, Verification Protocol,
   Reporting Protocol, Stop / Escalation Conditions, etc.).
5. **Pass loaded-skill evidence.** Include `Skills Referenced: task-handoff
   loaded=true` so the subagent template can rely on the pointer.
6. **Delegate** through the runtime-native surface (Copilot Task/agent,
   Claude `Agent` tool, OpenCode `task` + `@<agent>`, Codex child agent
   thread, Gemini root-session subagent call).

## Recommended Packet Form

| Task tag | Recommended form | Required expansion blocks beyond Required Minimum |
|---|---|---|
| `read-only-research` | short-form | Context Packet when sources are preselected |
| `content-quality-review` | short-form | Reporting Protocol |
| `code-edit` (≤ 2 files, no gates) | short-form | Context Packet when prior decisions matter |
| `code-edit` (> 2 files or shared boundary) | full-form | Goal & DoD · Scope · Context Packet · File Inventory · Workflow · Verification · Reporting |
| `code-change-build-gate` | full-form | Diff bucket · Required gates · Evidence schema · Routing · Wave · Reporting |
| `security-write` | full-form | Goal & DoD · Scope · Context Packet · File Inventory · Allowed Capabilities · Known Risks · Verification · Reporting · Clarification · Stop / Escalation |
| `bug-hunting` | full-form | Goal & DoD · Scope · Security Analysis · Context Packet · File Inventory · Allowed Capabilities · Known Risks · Reporting · Stop / Escalation |
| `mcp-write` | full-form | Goal & DoD · Scope · File Inventory · Allowed Capabilities · Known Risks · Reporting · Clarification · Stop / Escalation |
| `replication` | full-form | Goal & DoD · Scope · Context Packet · File Inventory · Verification · Reporting · Coordination |
| `release` | full-form | Goal & DoD · Verification · Reporting · Known Risks · Stop / Escalation |
| `docs-only` | short-form | Reporting Protocol when docs CI exists |
| `bug-fix` | full-form | Goal & DoD · Reproduction · Context Packet · Verification · Reporting |
| `instruction-memory-audit` | short-form | Findings list (direct-conflict, adapter-drift, duplicate-policy, skill-candidate, path-scoped-candidate, stale-generated-block) |

Use full-form whenever the task touches MCP, secrets, CI/release, dependency
manifests, generated scripts, ADRs, fan-out waves, or the Build Gate — even
if the table above suggests short-form.

For `code-edit`, `bug-fix`, `refactor`, and `code-review` tasks, also load the
`code-quality` skill and pass `Skills Referenced: code-quality loaded=true` so the
implementer and reviewer apply the project's conventions and maintainability
standards while working.

## Acceptance Checklist (consumer side — subagent runs this before work)

1. All 12 Required Minimum fields are present and non-empty.
2. `Context freshness` is explicit (`recent`, an `AGENTS.md` revision, or
   `reload`).
3. `File Inventory.to_modify` (when used) intersects only the subagent's
   Owned paths.
4. `File Inventory.to_read_only` (when used) does not include any path the
   subagent owns exclusively.
5. `Required approvals` lists every approval the task could trigger, or
   `none`.
6. `Verification Protocol` is provided for full-form tasks; otherwise the
   subagent falls back to `AGENTS.md` › Quality Gates.
7. `Reporting Protocol` matches the host's expected evidence shape.
8. `Constraints` and `Known Risks` mention every gate the subagent will
   touch.
9. `Coordination` lists wave siblings when `Dependencies / wave > 1`.
10. If the task changes generated agent, skill, memory, recommendation, or
    output-contract prose, the assignment names the expected Content
    Quality check or says `n/a`.
11. `Context Packet` is scoped to the subtask and does not paste full
    project memory.
12. `Allowed Capabilities` and `Skills Referenced` are runtime-neutral and
    do not ask the subagent to invent skills or mutate its own frontmatter.
13. Security-team tasks include authorization scope, affected asset, validation
    status, counterevidence, severity rationale, remediation verification
    need, and proof-gap reporting.
14. Build-gate tasks include the computed diff bucket, the required gates,
    and the evidence schema for the gate the subagent owns.

If a blocking check fails, return one consolidated `question_request` and
stop before the gated write.

## Reporting Template (consumer side — subagent emits)

```text
Outcome: <one sentence>
Files changed: <list with relative paths>
Evidence:
  - <test output>
  - <diff summary>
  - <other evidence per Reporting Protocol>
Gates touched: <list with status>
Task assignment quality: ok | warn | fail; form=<short|full>; missing=<fields|none>; questions=<count>
Content quality: ok | warn | fail | n/a; signals=<list|none>
Code quality: ok | warn | fail | n/a; signals=<list|none>
Security analysis: n/a | scope=<diff|repository|report|remediation|program>; authorization=<owned-code|approved-target|needs-approval>; validation=<confirmed|likely|needs-info|duplicate|not-reproducible|out-of-scope|mitigated|deferred>; severity=<P0|P1|P2|P3|n/a>; proof_gaps=<none|summary>
Build gate: n/a | bucket=<XS|S|M|L|XL>; gate=<build|unit|e2e|review|bug-hunt|validation>; status=<pass|fail|waived>; evidence=<summary>
Risks / escalations: <list or "none">
Question requests: none | <id>
Handoff status: accepted | completed | blocked | returned-to-orchestrator
Learning Check: none | proposed_new:<id> | proposed_update:<id> | deferred:<reason>
```

## Question Requests (consumer side fallback)

When a subagent cannot ask the human directly, it returns:

```yaml
question_request:
  id: <stable-kebab-id>
  asked_by: <subagent-name>
  reason: <why the answer is needed>
  question: <one concise question>
  choices: [<optional-choice-1>, <optional-choice-2>]
  default: <safe default or "none">
  urgency: blocking | nonblocking
  if_unanswered: skip | use_default | stop_before_write
  context:
    - <fact the orchestrator should show>
```

The host consumes the `question_request` through `AGENTS.md` › Human Input /
Question Protocol.

## Anti-patterns

- Subagent loads this skill to delegate to another subagent (it is host-only;
  subagents are executors).
- Host delegates without filling the Required Minimum 12 fields and relies on
  the subagent guard to catch it (slow, error-prone — fill the packet).
- Packet copies full `AGENTS.md` body into `Context Packet` (use a scoped
  slice; set `Context freshness: recent` when applicable).
- Subagent fakes Reporting Template fields it did not actually compute
  (return `Handoff status: blocked` with rationale instead).
- Subagent suppresses a `question_request` to avoid stopping work on a gated
  write (this re-enables the silent-write risk the gate exists to prevent).

## Output Contract

The host records `Task assignment quality: ok | warn | fail; form=<short|full>;
missing=<fields|none>; questions=<count>` in the integration report. The
subagent emits the Reporting Template above as its turn output.

